#### Integration with Shadcn/UI + Tailwind CSS (Both Patterns)

Tailwind CSS and Kendo UI operate in separate DOM territories and are never applied to the same elements. This is what makes the combination coherent rather than conflicting:

| DOM territory | Who styles it |
|---|---|
| Page shell, navigation, sidebar, forms, dialogs, filters, Shadcn/UI components | Tailwind CSS utility classes |
| `.k-grid`, `.k-table-td`, `.k-grid-header`, `.k-chart` and their children | Kendo's own `.k-*` CSS class system |

Tailwind's build-time purger only scans your source files for class names — it never touches Kendo's generated DOM. Kendo's `.k-*` classes are fully namespaced, so there are no utility class name collisions at the component level.

**However, Tailwind preflight is a real conflict risk that must be mitigated explicitly.**

Tailwind's preflight (`@tailwind base`) injects a global CSS reset using universal and element selectors (`*`, `table`, `th`, `td`, `button`, `input`, etc.). Because these selectors are not scoped to Tailwind utility classes, they apply to **every element in the DOM** — including Kendo's internal rendered elements. Kendo's Bootstrap-derived CSS builds on browser defaults that preflight strips, resulting in broken table cell borders, incorrect box-sizing, and missing button and input baseline styles inside the grid or chart.

**Required mitigation: scope Tailwind preflight to a wrapper element.**

Wrap all Shadcn/UI content in a dedicated container and configure Tailwind to scope its base styles to that wrapper, so preflight never enters the Kendo component boundary:

```css
/* globals.css — scope preflight to the Shadcn/UI layout wrapper */
@layer base {
  .tw-root, .tw-root * {
    /* Tailwind preflight resets applied only within this wrapper */
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }
}
```

```typescript
// tailwind.config.ts — disable global preflight; apply base styles via wrapper class
export default {
  corePlugins: {
    preflight: false,          // Disable Tailwind's global reset
  },
  // ...rest of config
}
```

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx — wrap all Shadcn/UI content; Kendo components render outside this boundary
export default function RootLayout({ children }) {
  return (
    <html>
      <body>
        <div className="tw-root">
          {/* All Shadcn/UI components, navigation, forms, layout */}
          {children}
        </div>
        {/* Kendo Grid or Chart rendered here — outside tw-root — is not affected by preflight */}
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

Alternatively, for simpler projects where Kendo is in a clearly isolated child route, disabling preflight globally (`corePlugins: { preflight: false }`) and manually adding only the resets Shadcn/UI requires is acceptable — but the scoped wrapper approach is preferred for long-term maintainability.

**The CSS custom property bridge is what produces visual consistency.** Without it, the Kendo component would look like a foreign element embedded in the page. The bridge maps Tailwind's design tokens (colours, radius, typography, spacing) into Kendo's parallel token system, so the grid or chart inherits the same visual language as the surrounding Shadcn/UI components — without Tailwind ever touching Kendo's internal DOM.

The combination would break down if teams attempted to:

- Apply Tailwind classes directly to Kendo's internal elements (`.k-grid td`) — those classes are not present in source files, so Tailwind's build-time purger removes them before they can take effect
- Use Kendo's general-purpose components (buttons, inputs, forms) for the same surfaces already covered by Shadcn/UI — this creates two competing styling systems on identical DOM patterns
- Add Kendo to `@gainwell/design-system` as a shared dependency — Kendo's CSS would load globally and produce specificity conflicts across the entire application

The guidance already prohibits all three. The architectural rule is: **Tailwind owns the boundary around the Kendo component; Kendo owns its interior; CSS custom properties bridge the design tokens across that boundary.**

Kendo Grid and Kendo Charts render their own DOM. Styling is aligned to the Tailwind design token system via Kendo's CSS custom property API:

```css
/* kendo-theme-override.css — imported in the affected module only */
.k-grid {
  --kendo-color-primary: hsl(var(--primary));
  --kendo-color-primary-hover: hsl(var(--primary) / 0.9);
  --kendo-base-bg: hsl(var(--background));
  --kendo-base-text: hsl(var(--foreground));
  --kendo-grid-header-bg: hsl(var(--muted));
  --kendo-grid-alt-bg: hsl(var(--muted) / 0.4);
  --kendo-border-radius-md: var(--radius);
  --kendo-font-family: var(--font-sans);
  --kendo-font-size: 0.875rem;
}
```

Radix UI components inside Kendo cell renderers:

```tsx
// Custom cell renderer — Radix components work inside Kendo cells
const ActionCellRenderer = ({ dataItem }: GridCellProps) => (
  <td>
    <DropdownMenu>
      <DropdownMenuTrigger asChild>
        <Button variant="ghost" size="icon" className="h-11 w-11">
          <MoreHorizontal className="h-4 w-4" />
          <span className="sr-only">Actions for claim {dataItem.claimId}</span>
        </Button>
      </DropdownMenuTrigger>
      <DropdownMenuContent align="end">
        <DropdownMenuItem>View</DropdownMenuItem>
        <DropdownMenuItem>Adjudicate</DropdownMenuItem>
      </DropdownMenuContent>
    </DropdownMenu>
  </td>
);
```

**Important:** Kendo components are installed and used **only within the module that qualifies for the exception**. They must not be added to `@gainwell/design-system` or any shared package.
